# Brain Coral — the boulder.
#
# The third silhouette in the reef set, and the one that does the structural
# work: the staghorn forks upward and the sea fan stands as a plane, so what is
# missing is *mass* — something low and heavy that the other two read against.
# A reef of nothing but spindly things is a thicket.
#
# **The grooves are colour, not geometry.** A brain coral is a dome with a
# meandering labyrinth cut into it, and modelling that as ridges would be a
# dozen sweeps trying to follow a curved surface — the exact thing `on=` cannot
# help with, since it snaps to the *closest* point and a groove is a path across
# one. `material_arc=` paints stripes *around* the tube instead, which costs no
# triangles beyond the vertex splits at the colour boundaries and cannot come
# adrift from the surface it is painted on.
#
# Two things about those arcs were learned from the render and are not
# negotiable. **Each arc is exactly one facet wide** — 30° on the twelve-sided
# dome, 36° on the ten-sided lobe. A 16° arc was the first attempt and it landed
# inside single facets, which snapped it to their edges and produced scattered
# square blotches rather than stripes; an arc narrower than a facet has nowhere
# to be. And **the arcs shift by exactly one facet on alternate rings**, so a
# stripe steps sideways as it climbs and steps back on the ring above. That
# zigzag is what turns six vertical bands into a labyrinth; a smooth few-degree
# drift per ring (the second attempt) reads as a barber's pole. The two groove
# materials alternate around the dome so the pattern is three-toned rather than
# stripes on a ball.
#
# **Two lobes, not one.** A single dome is a pebble. Real *Diploria* grows as
# fused heads, so a smaller second dome sits `on=` the first — flush by
# construction — and breaks the outline where the two meet. The second lobe is
# deliberately off-centre in both X and Z: put it on the mirror plane and the
# model reads as one symmetric mound with a bump, which is a pebble again.
#
# **It is authored wide.** `BlockModels` scales a model by its height alone, so
# a squat model arrives *large* — a dome 1.8x wider than it is tall becomes 1.8
# cells across if it is scaled to a full cell. `main.js` therefore gives this
# kind a height well under one cell (see MODELLED_PLANTS), and the check below
# pins the aspect so that number stays the right one.
#
# Gold, where the staghorn is pink and the fan purple. Three corals in one reef
# have to be separable at a glance in water that is already eating the blue end
# of everything.

model coral_brain
  height 0.46
  style smooth

palette
  Head   #d9a63e
  Groove #a3781f
  Ridge  #f0cd74
  Foot   #7a5a2a

skeleton
  root rock at 0.0
  bone head parent=rock dir=up len=0.56
  # The second lobe. Off the mirror plane on both axes — see the header.
  bone lobe parent=rock offset=(0.38,0.035,0.14) dir=up pitch=3 tilt=-6 len=0.38

parts
  # The main head. Widest at a quarter of its height and barely narrower at half
  # — a brain coral overhangs its own base slightly, which is what makes it read
  # as grown rather than as a hill of sand.
  #
  # `material_arc` angles are measured with 0° on the `w` axis and 90° on `d`,
  # and every ring shifts its four arcs by a few more degrees than the ring
  # below. Over the height of the dome that is roughly a sixth of a turn, which
  # is enough for a stripe to visibly wander without spiralling.
  loft dome bones=rock..head material=Head sides=12
    ring 0.00 w=0.62 d=0.56 material=Foot
    ring 0.16 w=0.86 d=0.78 material=Head material_arc=Groove:0-30,Ridge:60-90,Groove:120-150,Ridge:180-210,Groove:240-270,Ridge:300-330
    ring 0.40 w=0.94 d=0.86 material_arc=Groove:30-60,Ridge:90-120,Groove:150-180,Ridge:210-240,Groove:270-300,Ridge:330-360
    ring 0.62 w=0.90 d=0.82 material_arc=Groove:0-30,Ridge:60-90,Groove:120-150,Ridge:180-210,Groove:240-270,Ridge:300-330
    ring 0.82 w=0.74 d=0.68 material_arc=Groove:30-60,Ridge:90-120,Groove:150-180,Ridge:210-240,Groove:270-300,Ridge:330-360
    ring 0.94 w=0.48 d=0.44 material_arc=Groove:0-30,Ridge:60-90,Groove:120-150,Ridge:180-210,Groove:240-270,Ridge:300-330
    ring 1.00 w=0.20 d=0.18 material_arc=Groove:30-60,Ridge:90-120,Groove:150-180,Ridge:210-240,Groove:270-300,Ridge:330-360
    cap start=flat end=dome

  # The second head. Same construction, two thirds the size, and pushed far
  # enough out in +X that it sits *entirely* to one side of the mirror plane.
  #
  # Two things forced that distance, and both are worth the width it costs. The
  # lint counts a part straddling X=0 lopsidedly as a broken symmetry claim, and
  # a lobe half-overlapping the axis is exactly that. And `on=dome`, which was
  # the first way this was mounted, snaps the part's *origin* onto the dome's
  # surface — for a lobe rooted at the seabed that meant sinking its foot 0.14
  # below the ground. Two heads growing from the same rock and overlapping is
  # the honest description; the fusion comes from the overlap, not from `on=`.
  loft lump bones=lobe..lobe material=Head sides=10
    ring 0.00 w=0.40 d=0.36 material=Foot
    ring 0.18 w=0.58 d=0.52 material=Head material_arc=Ridge:0-36,Groove:72-108,Ridge:144-180,Groove:216-252,Ridge:288-324
    ring 0.48 w=0.62 d=0.56 material_arc=Ridge:36-72,Groove:108-144,Ridge:180-216,Groove:252-288,Ridge:324-360
    ring 0.78 w=0.52 d=0.47 material_arc=Ridge:0-36,Groove:72-108,Ridge:144-180,Groove:216-252,Ridge:288-324
    ring 0.94 w=0.30 d=0.27 material_arc=Ridge:36-72,Groove:108-144,Ridge:180-216,Groove:252-288,Ridge:324-360
    ring 1.00 w=0.12 d=0.11 material_arc=Ridge:0-36,Groove:72-108,Ridge:144-180,Groove:216-252,Ridge:288-324
    cap start=flat end=dome

checks
  # Sits on the seabed on both lobes.
  assert bottom(dome) < 0.01
  # A boulder: wide and low. This is the number `main.js` picks the kind's
  # height against, so it is the one that must not drift.
  assert width / height in 1.3..2.2
  # The dome overhangs its own foot rather than tapering from it like a cone.
  assert width(dome) > 0.85
  # Two lobes, one clearly smaller, and the small one lower — a second head the
  # same size as the first is a dumbbell.
  assert width(lump) < width(dome) * 0.8
  assert top(lump) < top(dome)
  # ...and it is genuinely off the mirror plane, which is what stops the model
  # reading as one symmetric mound.
  assert x(lump) > 0.08
  assert abs(z(lump)) > 0.04
  # The grooves survive. `materials` counts what actually reached the mesh, so
  # if a `material_arc` is ever mistyped into nothing this fails rather than
  # quietly rendering a plain gold ball.
  assert materials == 4
  assert parts == 2
  assert tris < 900
  measure aspect width / height
  measure lumpfoot bottom(lump)
